Hi Andrew.

Thank you for the reply.

Yes, that is true.

But, I thought that :

ps[j] = exp(poisson_lpmf(K[j] | lambda) + binomial_lpmf( n_detections[i] | n_samples[i], theta ));

Would be equal to 0 in that case. Some mucking about with print suggests that exp(negative_infinity()) does return an expected 0.

And, then would not be a problem when adding to target because I sum over K then take the log of the sum, and add that to target:

total = sum( ps );
target += log(total);

I realise that is contravening the manual's suggestion of using log_sum_exp(). But, I think the implementation of that function (as suggested by the manual) would not work in this case.

But, I took the following approach in the code below. I introduced an if/else clause, stating that if binomial_lpmf is Inf/-Inf, then return 0, otherwise, calculate the log-likelihood. However, this still fails to run:

learn_hpois <- function(data, lower_N = 0, upper_N = 20, iter = 2000, warmup = 1000, chains = 4) {
 K <- seq( lower_N, upper_N )
 data_list <- list(
                    n_points = length( K ),
                    n_ind = nrow( data ),
                    n_detections = data$w,
                    n_samples = data$n_samples,
                    K = K)
 hpois_model <- '
 data{
   int<lower=1> n_points; //
    int<lower=1> n_ind; // number of surveyed birds
   int<lower=0> n_detections[n_ind]; // vector of number of detections
   int<lower=1> n_samples[n_ind]; // vector of number of tests
   int K[n_points];
 }
 parameters{
   real<lower=0> lambda; // distribution of abundance
   real<lower=0, upper=1> p_detect; // detection probability
 }
 model{
   real ps[n_points];
   real total;
   real theta;
   real diff;
   lambda ~ exponential(1); // prior on lambda
   p_detect ~ uniform(0, 1); // flat prior on detection prob
   diff = (1 - p_detect);
   for(i in 1:n_ind) {
     for(j in 1:n_points){
       theta = (1 - pow( diff, K[j] ));

       if( is_inf( binomial_lpmf( n_detections[i] | n_samples[i], theta ) ) ) {
         ps[j] = 0;
       } else {

         ps[j] = exp(poisson_lpmf(K[j] | lambda) + binomial_lpmf( n_detections[i] | n_samples[i], theta ));
       }
     }
     total = sum( ps );
     target += log(total);
   }
 }
 generated quantities {
   real psi; // probability of occupancy
   psi = 1 - exp( -lambda );
 }
 '
 fit <- rstan::stan( model_code = hpois_model,
                      data = data_list,
                     iter = iter,
                      warmup = warmup,

                     chains = chains)
  return( fit )
}

Perhaps not a model for Stan?

I should also mention that I have tried other version of this if/else clause (e.g., if theta == 0 && n_detections > 0 || theta ==1 && n_detections < n_samples). Still to no avail.

Notice that here, I removed the 'init' parameter from the fitting function. It seems that Stan was having trouble initialising with the values I chose. But, it seems to be with any value.

Thank you once again.

Anders.

PS: The error seems to have changed to:
[298] "Rejecting initial value:"
[299] "  Log probability evaluates to log(0), i.e. negative infinity."
[300] "  Stan can't start sampling from this initial value."
[301] "Initialization between (-2, 2) failed after 100 attempts. "
[302] " Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model."
[303] "Error : "

But, I constrained the parameters appropriately.

